ignore-value silently stores dead ignores for any rule outside the 6-rule value whitelist

## Summary

`hooks ignore-value <rule> "<value>"` reports success and persists the entry for **any** rule, but the entry is inert unless the rule is one of six. `extractFindingIgnoreValue()` returns `''` for every other rule, and `isIgnoredFindingValue()` bails on empty values, so the stored ignore can never match.

`hooks status` lists the dead entries alongside working ones, so there is no way to tell them apart. The only symptom is that the finding keeps coming back.

## Where

`skills/impeccable/scripts/hook-lib.mjs`

```js
export function extractFindingIgnoreValue(finding) {
  if (!finding || typeof finding !== 'object') return '';
  const rule = normalizeIgnoreRule(finding.antipattern);
  const directValueRules = new Set([
    'overused-font',
    'bounce-easing',
    'design-system-font',
    'design-system-color',
    'design-system-radius',
    'design-system-font-size',
  ]);
  if (!directValueRules.has(rule)) return '';   // <- everything else
  return normalizeIgnoreValue(extractFindingIgnoreValueRaw(finding, rule));
}
```

and

```js
function isIgnoredFindingValue(finding, ignoreValues) {
  ...
  const value = extractFindingIgnoreValue(finding);
  return ignoreValues.some((entry) => {
    if (entry.rule !== rule) return false;
    const wildcardValue = entry.value === '*';
    if (!wildcardValue && (!value || !ignoreValueMatches(rule, entry.value, value))) return false;
    //                     ^^^^^^ always true for non-whitelisted rules
```

Note that `extractFindingIgnoreValueRaw()` right below it already reads `finding.ignoreValue || finding.value`, then falls back to parsing `finding.detail` / `finding.snippet`. It looks capable of handling more rules than the whitelist admits.

## Reproduction

```js
import { extractFindingIgnoreValue, filterFindings } from './skills/impeccable/scripts/hook-lib.mjs';

const finding = {
  antipattern: 'cramped-padding',
  snippet: '<dl> "facts": children flush against border-top on top (no inset)',
  value:   '<dl> "facts": children flush against border-top on top (no inset)',
  ignoreValue: '<dl> "facts": children flush against border-top on top (no inset)',
  file: 'index.html',
};

console.log(extractFindingIgnoreValue(finding));
// ''  — even though the finding carries the value in three separate fields

const exact = { ignoreValues: [{ rule: 'cramped-padding', value: finding.snippet }] };
console.log(filterFindings([finding], '', '.html', exact).length);
// 1  — expected 0

const wildcard = { ignoreValues: [{ rule: 'cramped-padding', value: '*', files: ['index.html'] }] };
console.log(filterFindings([finding], '', '.html', wildcard).length);
// 0  — the only form that works
```

Same result for `marquee`, and by inspection for every rule outside the six.

## Why it is easy to misdiagnose

The per-edit `PostToolUse` pass only surfaces its immediate tier, while the `Stop` pass runs the full rule set. For a deep-pass-only rule such as `cramped-padding`, adding a dead exact-value ignore looks like it worked: `PostToolUse` goes quiet (it was never going to report that rule anyway) and `hooks status` shows the entry.

The finding then reappears at every `Stop`, which reads as a `PostToolUse` / `Stop` divergence rather than an inert ignore. I chased that false lead for a while before finding the whitelist.

## Impact

The agent-facing hook message instructs the agent to run

```
hook-admin.mjs ignore-value <rule> "<value>" --reason "..."
```

with the pair shown on the finding line. For any rule outside the six that is a silent no-op, so the agent believes the exception is recorded, tells the user so, and the same findings return every turn. In my case 14 stored ignores were dead.

## Suggested fixes

Any one of these would remove the trap:

1. Let `extractFindingIgnoreValue()` fall through to `extractFindingIgnoreValueRaw()` for all rules, using `finding.ignoreValue || finding.value || finding.snippet`.
2. Have `hook-admin ignore-value` refuse a non-wildcard value for a rule that cannot extract one, and point at `"*" --file <glob>` instead.
3. Failing both, mark such entries in `hooks status` (for example `(inert: rule has no extractable value)`) so the dead ones are visible.

## Environment

- impeccable 4.1.1 (plugin install)
- Claude Code, Linux (WSL2), node 24.13
- Rules hit: `cramped-padding`, `marquee`

## Workaround

`hooks ignore-value <rule> "*" --file <path>` works, at the cost of disabling the rule for the whole file.

Critique snapshots have no close path, so polish inherits a stale backlog

## Summary

A critique snapshot is written as an archive and then presented as a live to-do list, with no way to mark it answered.

`$impeccable polish` and the no-argument routing menu read the latest file under `.impeccable/critique/` as a fix backlog. Nothing deletes that file, sets a resolved flag, or checks its `timestamp` against the target. `.gitignore` already treats per-run snapshots as local artifacts (`ignore.md` is the exception), so the intended lifetime is "this session" — but the skill still treats whatever is on disk as current work.

## What it costs

In [erikpr1994/peakhealth#992](https://github.com/erikpr1994/peakhealth/issues/992) a 2026-08-08 critique of the routine builder (score 21/40, seven Priority Issues) stayed in the tree after every finding had been fixed on `main`. A later session spent a full sub-agent pass (~200k tokens) proving each finding stale before it could act.

`.gitignore` would have stopped that file being committed. It would not have stopped polish from inheriting a leftover local snapshot, and it does not stop a consumer who *does* commit snapshots (this one did, because the consumer gitignore had mocks/review but not critique).

## Cause

`critique.md` names the snapshot an "archive/backlog for future commands". `polish.md` says: if `latest` exits 0, incorporate the P0/P1 findings. `routing.md` says: a low score or non-zero `p0`/`p1` → offer polish. `critique-storage.mjs` can `write` / `latest` / `trend` and has no close path.

The snapshot therefore outlives the work it described. A later run cannot tell "still open" from "already fixed" without re-deriving every finding against the current code.

## Suggested fix

Close a snapshot by deleting it. That is the cheapest resolved state: `latest` already treats absence as "no backlog", gitignore already wants snapshots off disk, and a new `$impeccable critique` writes a new file.

A `close` subcommand on the helper so agents do not hand-`rm` a glob.

### `skill/scripts/critique-storage.mjs`

Header, add the command:

```
 *   node critique-storage.mjs close <slug>
```

Export:

```js
/** Delete the latest snapshot for `slug`. Returns the path removed, or null. */
export function closeSnapshot(slug, { cwd = process.cwd() } = {}) {
  const latest = readLatestSnapshot(slug, { cwd });
  if (!latest) return null;
  fs.unlinkSync(latest.path);
  return latest.path;
}
```

CLI, next to `latest`:

```js
    case 'close': {
      const slug = coerceSlug(args[0]);
      if (!slug) { process.stderr.write('usage: close <slug-or-target>\n'); process.exit(1); }
      const closed = closeSnapshot(slug);
      if (!closed) { process.exit(2); }
      process.stdout.write(`${closed}\n`);
      return;
    }
```

Update the usage line in `default` to include `close`.

### `skill/reference/critique.md`

Purpose, last sentence:

```
- The chat response is the primary deliverable; the snapshot is an archive/backlog for future commands.
+ The chat response is the primary deliverable; the snapshot is an archive of that run. Later commands may read it as a starting backlog only while its findings still describe the code.
```

New section after **Persist the Snapshot**, before **Ask the User**:

```
### Close the Snapshot

A snapshot is open while the file exists. When every Priority Issue is fixed, declined, or written to `.impeccable/critique/ignore.md`, close it:

```bash
node {{scripts_path}}/critique-storage.mjs close "<resolved target>"
```

Exit 0 prints the path removed. Exit 2 means none existed. A new `$impeccable critique` writes a new snapshot. Do not leave a closed snapshot on disk: polish and routing will read it as an open backlog.
```

### `skill/reference/polish.md`

After the `latest` command:

```
- Exit 0 returns the latest snapshot; incorporate relevant P0/P1 findings and name the snapshot read. Exit 2 means none exists. Perform an independent pass either way.
+ Exit 0 returns the latest snapshot. Treat it as a starting backlog only while its findings still describe the target; when the target's source has moved on since `timestamp`, re-run `$impeccable critique` rather than inheriting the file. Exit 2 means none exists. Perform an independent pass either way.
```

In **5. Verify and finish**, after the source-diff sentence:

```
+ When this pass clears every Priority Issue it took from a snapshot, close that snapshot:
+
+ ```bash
+ node {{scripts_path}}/critique-storage.mjs close "<resolved target>"
+ ```
```

### `skill/reference/routing.md`

```
- `critique.latest` with a low `score` or non-zero `p0` / `p1` → `polish` (it reads that snapshot as its backlog), or re-run `critique` if the snapshot looks stale.
+ `critique.latest` with a low `score` or non-zero `p0` / `p1` → `polish` (it reads that snapshot as its backlog) only while the snapshot still describes the target; re-run `critique` when the target has been committed since the snapshot's `timestamp`.
```

### Tests

In `tests/critique-storage.test.mjs`, next to the `latest` CLI case:

```js
  it('close subcommand deletes the latest snapshot and exits 0', () => {
    writeSnapshot({ slug: 'index-astro', meta: { total_score: 20 }, body: 'open', cwd });
    const r = spawnSync(process.execPath, [SCRIPT, 'close', 'index-astro'], {
      cwd,
      encoding: 'utf-8',
    });
    assert.equal(r.status, 0, `stderr: ${r.stderr}`);
    assert.equal(readLatestSnapshot('index-astro', { cwd }), null);
  });

  it('close subcommand exits 2 when no snapshot exists', () => {
    const r = spawnSync(process.execPath, [SCRIPT, 'close', 'never-written'], {
      cwd,
      encoding: 'utf-8',
    });
    assert.equal(r.status, 2);
  });
```

And a unit test beside `writeSnapshot + readLatestSnapshot` that `closeSnapshot` returns the path and leaves `readLatestSnapshot` null.

## Alternatives considered

1. **Frontmatter `resolved: true` (or per-finding status).** Keeps trend history on disk. `latest` and routing then have to filter. More surface for the model to forget to set.
2. **`impeccable doctor` flags a snapshot whose target has commits after `timestamp`.** Useful as a mention, but doctor does not read critiques today, and a mention still leaves polish inheriting the file until someone acts.

Delete-on-close is the one `latest` already understands. Trend history is the last five snapshots that have not been closed; a closed run is one you can re-run.

Happy to open a PR if you would like it — following the issue-first rule and waiting for your go-ahead.

enable-udp-punch and enable-ipv6-punch default to OFF on the public servers (is_public() not port-aware, regression from #13701)

### Summary

On the **public** rendezvous servers, `enable-udp-punch` and `enable-ipv6-punch` both resolve to disabled when unset — the inverse of what the code intends. This is a regression introduced by #13701.

### Root cause

Three pieces interact:

**1.** `Config::get_rendezvous_server()` always returns `host:port` (hbb_common `src/config.rs`):

```rust
if !rendezvous_server.contains(':') {
    rendezvous_server = format!("{rendezvous_server}:{RENDEZVOUS_PORT}");
}
rendezvous_server
```

**2.** `is_public()` matches URL shapes only (`src/common.rs`):

```rust
pub fn is_public(url: &str) -> bool {
    let url = url.to_ascii_lowercase();
    url.contains("rustdesk.com/") || url.ends_with("rustdesk.com")
}
```

**3.** `get_local_option()` is the only caller that passes a `host:port` rather than a URL (`src/common.rs`):

```rust
pub fn get_local_option(key: &str) -> String {
    let v = LocalConfig::get_option(key);
    if key == keys::OPTION_ENABLE_UDP_PUNCH || key == keys::OPTION_ENABLE_IPV6_PUNCH {
        if v.is_empty() {
            if !is_public(&Config::get_rendezvous_server()) {
                return "N".to_owned();
            }
        }
    }
    v
}
```

`"rs-ny.rustdesk.com:21116"` neither ends with `rustdesk.com` nor contains `rustdesk.com/`, so `is_public` returns `false`, `!is_public` is `true`, and both options resolve to `"N"`.

The guard clearly means *"default off only when self-hosted"*. On the public infrastructure it does the opposite.

### Regression point

#13701 (commit `a78a803a`, 2025-12-02) tightened the check:

```diff
 pub fn is_public(url: &str) -> bool {
-    url.contains("rustdesk.com")
+    url.contains("rustdesk.com/") || url.ends_with("rustdesk.com")
 }
```

| Input | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| `rs-ny.rustdesk.com:21116` | `true` | `false` |
| `https://api.rustdesk.com/v1` | `true` | `true` |
| `rustdesk.comhello.com` | `true` | `false` |

The intent was correct — eliminating false positives such as `rustdesk.comhello.com` and `rustdesk.computer.com`. But only URL-shaped inputs were considered, and `get_local_option` was left passing a `host:port`. I compared `get_local_option` at `a78a803a` and its parent: identical, untouched by that PR, so the defaults flipped silently underneath an existing caller.

The test suite added in the same PR covers eight URL forms and six negatives, with no `host:port` case — which is why this stayed green.

### Impact

Every user on the public servers who has never explicitly toggled these options gets UDP and IPv6 hole punching disabled, and silently falls back to relay where a direct connection was possible.

In my own case this was the difference between relaying via `ovh-fr2` and a direct IPv6 connection in ~25 ms between two Israeli endpoints that both have native IPv6. Setting `enable-ipv6-punch = 'Y'` explicitly fixed it immediately.

The GUI reflects the computed value faithfully, so the checkbox simply appears unchecked, giving no hint that a default is being inverted.

### Suggested fix

Strip the port at the call site rather than loosening `is_public` again:

```rust
let server = Config::get_rendezvous_server();
let host = server.rsplit_once(':').map(|(h, _)| h).unwrap_or(&server);
if !is_public(host) {
```

`rsplit_once` so a bracketed IPv6 literal degrades gracefully. Worth adding `assert!(is_public_host("rs-ny.rustdesk.com:21116"))` to `test_is_public` to prevent recurrence.

Related: #15920 (separate defect on the same UDP punch path).

[Linux][Flatpak] File transfer always fails with "No active console user logged on": `run_loginctl` passes the whole command line to `flatpak-spawn --host` as one argument

### Bug Description

When the controlled side runs the Flatpak build (`com.rustdesk.RustDesk`), **Transfer file** always fails with:

> No active console user logged on, please connect and logon first.

even though a user is logged in on the desktop, and remote control of the same machine works. This looks like the same problem as #11827, which was closed without a root cause. The cause is below.

### Root cause

The controlled side fills `PeerInfo.username` from `get_active_username()` → `get_values_of_seat0()` → `run_loginctl()` (`libs/base/src/platform/linux.rs`). Under Flatpak, `run_loginctl` does:

```rust
if std::env::var("FLATPAK_ID").is_ok() {
    let mut l_args = CMD_LOGINCTL.to_string();
    if let Some(a) = args.as_ref() {
        l_args = format!("{} {}", l_args, a.join(" "));
    }
    let res = std::process::Command::new("flatpak-spawn")
        .args(vec![String::from("--host"), l_args])
        .output();
    if res.is_ok() {
        return res;
    }
}
```

This causes two problems:

1. **The whole command is passed as one argv element.** For `is_active(sid)`, the host is asked to execute a program literally named `loginctl show-session -p State <sid>`, which doesn't exist. Because `.output()` still returns `Ok` (with a non-zero exit status and empty stdout), the fallback never runs. `is_active()` is therefore always false, no seat0 session is found, `username` is empty, and the client shows the error from `handle_peer_info` (`ui_session_interface.rs`).
2. **The Flathub build lacks `--talk-name=org.freedesktop.Flatpak`,** so `flatpak-spawn --host` fails even for the plain `loginctl` call. Again `Ok` is returned with empty stdout.

Remote control doesn't hit this, because it doesn't require `pi.username` to be non-empty.

### Reproduction of the root cause (on a host with RustDesk Flatpak installed)

```
# 1) Without the Flatpak talk-name (current Flathub permissions):
$ flatpak run --command=flatpak-spawn com.rustdesk.RustDesk --host loginctl list-sessions --no-legend
Portal call failed: org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.ServiceUnknown
Hint: --host only works when the Flatpak is allowed to talk to org.freedesktop.Flatpak

# 2) With the talk-name, plain `loginctl` works:
$ flatpak run --talk-name=org.freedesktop.Flatpak --command=flatpak-spawn com.rustdesk.RustDesk --host loginctl list-sessions --no-legend
<lists sessions correctly, including the active seat0 session>

# 3) But the single-argument form RustDesk uses for is_active() fails:
$ flatpak run --talk-name=org.freedesktop.Flatpak --command=flatpak-spawn com.rustdesk.RustDesk --host "loginctl show-session -p State <sid>"
Portal call failed: Failed to start command: Failed to execute child process "loginctl show-session -p State <sid>" (No such file or directory)
```

So even after granting `flatpak override --user --talk-name=org.freedesktop.Flatpak com.rustdesk.RustDesk`, file transfer still fails because of problem 1.

### Suggested fix

Pass the program and its arguments as separate argv elements, and only accept the result if the command succeeded:

```rust
if std::env::var("FLATPAK_ID").is_ok() {
    let mut cmd = std::process::Command::new("flatpak-spawn");
    cmd.arg("--host").arg(CMD_LOGINCTL.as_str());
    if let Some(a) = args.as_ref() {
        cmd.args(a);
    }
    if let Ok(out) = cmd.output() {
        if out.status.success() {
            return Ok(out);
        }
    }
}
```

And in the Flathub manifest (`com.rustdesk.RustDesk`), add `--talk-name=org.freedesktop.Flatpak` to `finish-args`. Without it, the `flatpak-spawn --host` path can never work. If that permission is considered too broad, an alternative is to query `org.freedesktop.login1` over the system bus (with `--system-talk-name=org.freedesktop.login1`) instead of shelling out to `loginctl`.

### How to Reproduce

1. Install RustDesk from Flathub on two Ubuntu machines (GNOME, Wayland).
2. From A, connect to B with remote control. This works.
3. From A, open **Transfer file** to B.
4. The remote file list appears briefly, then the error dialog is shown. Clicking OK closes the window.

